\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again, dealers seek clarity  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets  \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2005\par 14:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (New quotes, background)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Aug 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday on Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market it closely controls, paying slightly more for dollars than before and sowing new confusion among dealers seeking clear policy guidelines.  \par \par \uc2 The bank bought dollars at 4.99 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slightly higher than 4.98 at the previous three sessions.  \par \par \uc2 There was no explanation for the slight rise in the dollar against the Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a day after the resignation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy chairman Serhiy Yaryomenko, an opponent of calls to liberalise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "Why did the dollar rise? This is a question troubling us all. It's difficult to understand -- perhaps this is what they wanted," said one dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants expressed unease over the unpredictable nature of rates and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's apparent reluctance to explain its policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Now that Yaryomenko is gone there is absolutely no clarity on what policy is to follow," said another dealer. "Everyone is waiting for the long-promised liberalisation of the market but so far there is no sign of any decision by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars at 5.01 for several weeks beginning in late April after engineering a three percent rise in a single day that rattled confidence in the market. Last week, it bought dollars more cheaply, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise gently when it broke through the barrier of 5.0 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers have expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman Volodymyr Stelmakh to outline policy since President Viktor Yushchenko told him to draft proposals to ease tough restrictions on banks.  \par \par \uc2 WAITING FOR CLARIFICATION  \par \par \uc2 But there has been no sign of any impending statement. Stelmakh attended a fair in central Ukraine with Yushchenko on Wednesday with no indication of his programme in coming days.  \par \par \uc2 "This increases the chances of a more liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, which would mean a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the longer run," Goldman Sachs said in a note of Yaryomenko's departure. "But there is a lack of clarity about what is exactly happening."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers, ministers and the International Monetary Fund have called on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to liberalise the market and control money supply to curb inflation -- running at about 15 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say that if banks were allowed to conduct arbitrage deals or use other instruments, market imbalances would be avoided. The bank has failed since May to implement promises to ease restrictions, including a ban on banks buying and selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the same day.  \par \par \uc2 Stelmakh told Kommersant Ukraine daily his deputy's resignation was dictated by the "demands of our times" as he had "too broad a view of the functions of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "He believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should work like the U.S. Federal Reserve, looking after various sectors," Stelmakh told the newspaper. "Under the constitution, our concerns are limited to stability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prices".  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Stelmakh said, "should concentrate its efforts on preventing a spiral of inflation, though that is difficult with the current socially-oriented budget".  \par \par \uc2 The head of the Association of Ukrainian banks, Oleksander Suhonyako, told the same newspaper he had asked Stelmakh to give a "clear notion of the prospects, of what we can expect".  \par \par \uc2 Jacques Mounier, chief executive of Calyon Bank Ukraine, also issued an impassioned plea for clarity.  \par \par \uc2 "(Federal Reserve Chairman Alan) Greenspan ... also uses different levers, but does it in such as way as to avoid disruption to the market," he told the weekly Bizness.  \par \par \uc2 "The leaders of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should learn from Greenspan about adopting decisions and making them public."  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-UKRAINE-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050817e18h00183\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
