\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  rate hike,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  has markets confused  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Olena Horodetska  \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2005\par 15:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout after rate rise)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent contradictory signals to the markets on Tuesday, resuming dollar purchases to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while raising its key discount rate.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement suggested the rate hike was designed to curb inflation. But analysts say freeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only effective way of combating price pressure and that Tuesday's two decisions were likely to cancel each other out.  \par \par \uc2 "They have been consistent in their inconsistency. It is difficult to understand what they are trying to achieve," said Tim Ash, emerging markets economist at Bear Stearns in London.  \par \par \uc2 "What investors need is consistency across the board. But we are not getting a clear message."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to its highest since 1999 this week, breaking through the level of 5 to the dollar. Dealers said deals struck on Tuesday ranged from 4.90 to 4.96/97.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ignored the rise on the interbank market throughout Monday and until the very end of trading on Tuesday. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the session officially closed, buying dollars at 5.01.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably, the decision was very difficult," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. "Banks are queuing and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying dollars at its usual rate of 5.01 to the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The bank also raised its key discount rate to 9.5 percent from 9 percent from Aug. 10. Obligatory reserve requirements for commercial banks were raised to 8 percent from 7 percent from Sept. 1 on \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement said the rate decision was adopted "after analysing macroeconomic and monetary indicators and also taking into consideration increasing inflation pressures".  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials declined requests for comment.  \par \par \uc2 TACKLING INFLATION  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing a policy dilemma this year.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund and government officials have singled out inflation as Ukraine's major macroeconomic problem. The IMF has urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb money supply growth and loosen its grip on the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has for months been printing \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy out dollars - about $5 billion this year. M3 broad money supply rose by 26.5 percent in the first seven months.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unwilling to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate too much as it will hurt exporters, a major driver of Ukraine's economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 The pace of economic growth slumped to 4 percent in the first half in annual terms, its lowest level in five years.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation pressures are rising. Monthly inflation reached 0.3 percent in July, exceeding the government forecast. Annual inflation nearly doubled to 14.2 percent in seven months.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it is a step in the right direction that they want to do something about containing inflationary pressures," said Jens Nystedt, chief economist EMEA at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But at the end of the day their main tool remains the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we see this as the starting shot for the second appreciation step that we have been calling for ever since the first one. A significant appreciation is still in the cards."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadily to buy the dollar at 5.01 since late April when it allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise by about 3 percent in a single day. The move, criticised by politicians and consumers, jolted market confidence.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said freeing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was the ultimate solution for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which acts a clearance house for the market as commercial banks are not allowed to buy and sell hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the same trading day.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we should not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market liberalisation this year," said Ihor Solntsev, first deputy chairman of Forum Bank. "In our country there is a lot of potential instability. Neither the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nor clients want to create more instability." (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases in London and Douglas Busvine in Moscow)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-UKRAINE-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050809e189001er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{