\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  resumes dollar sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2005\par 14:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds dealer's quote, details)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, June 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, selling dollars at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly controls the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and acts as a clearance house as commercial banks are not allowed both to sell and buy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a single trading day. It was buying dollars at 5.01 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "Today buyers have won," said a dealer at a Western bank. "Everybody knows fewer dollars will be coming into the country but there is no global change of trend yet. The difference between supply and demand is not that big and we could see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars one day and selling on the other."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market in April by allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise by about 3 percent in a single trading day. Since then it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to defend its new target rate of 5.05 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund and Western economists have called on Ukraine to allow greater rate flexibility to fight price rises. Inflation has doubled to 14 percent year-to-year in the first five months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko signalled on Friday that she favoured further exchange rate liberalisation, saying it was natural for market forces to set the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate.  \par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have repeatedly said they would not allow sharp changes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remaining steady until the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says Ukraine's financial stability is still too fragile to relinquish control over the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Volatility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a very sensitive issue in Ukraine where many lost their life savings due to botched monetary reforms in the early 1990s.  \par \par \uc2 With eyes on a parliamentary election due in March 2006, officials are trying to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050613e16d0014w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
