\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Euro decline buffets C.Europe, lira steady  \b0\par\par\uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2005\par 11:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 1 (Reuters) - Sharp euro losses made for choppy trade in central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, though they broadly held to established ranges as investors continued to mull the fallout from France's rejection of the EU constitution.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit 7-1/2 month lows against the dollar <EUR=> around $1.2270 with another leg down coming on the back of media reports a possible failure of European Monetary Union was discussed at a high-level meeting of German officials.  \par \par \uc2 A German finance ministry spokesman later dismissed such talk as "absurd", though the market had already been in bearish mode on expectations the Dutch would join France in voting "no" to the constitution in a referendum on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The impact of euro moves on central Europe was mixed, with the Polish zloty recovering some lost ground, while the Hungarian forint and Czech and Slovak crowns nudged lower.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro/dollar story is an important one, but it's tricky, the correlations go up and down," said Jeff Gable, emerging markets analyst at Barclays Capital.  \par \par \uc2 "By and large, I remain reluctant to bet for further central European strength in an environment of significant euro weakness."  \par \par \uc2 The zloty recovered some lost ground after tripping up on Tuesday on news of a sharp slowdown in Polish growth to 2.1 percent year-on-year in the first quarter, from 4.0 percent in the final quarter of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "Polish GDP was disappointing, but there's no sign that its weak euro zone growth that is causing this...nothing in the data suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the wrong level or that weak German or Italian growth is causing the slowdown," said Gable.  \par \par \uc2 By 0847 GMT, the zloty traded at 4.1491 per euro <EURPLN=> from 4.1532 late on Tuesday and at 3.3778 per dollar <PLN=> from 3.3622.  \par \par \uc2 LIRA STEADY  \par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira held to tight ranges against the dollar, continuing to show resilience in the face of mounting uncertainty over its EU membership prospects following France's rejection of the constitution.  \par \par \uc2 EU officials have been quick to reassure Turkey that its accession talks, due to start on Oct. 3, would not be affected, but some analysts have still been surprised by the relatively calm reaction on Turkish markets.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets have effectively discounted the fact that come October the Turks are going to begin their accession talks. But I think there's a lot of potential noise surrounding the beginning of the talks," said Dwyfor Evans, emerging markets analyst at Bank of America.  \par \par \uc2 "I expect the political parties in Germany to take quite a an anti-Turkish line ahead of the German elections. That comes a couple of weeks before the accession talks."  \par \par \uc2 The lira traded at 1.3640 to the dollar on the interbank market <IYIX=> from 1.3570 at Tuesday's close.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for the first time in two months on Tuesday, to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable around 5.05 <UAH=>.  \par \par \uc2 The bank had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most of May to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it allowed it to appreciate to 5.05, from 5.19 in a single day.  \par \par \uc2 Rising import demand had shifted the trend in favour of a stronger dollar in recent sessions, prompting the move from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at 5.0610 per dollar on Wednesday, according to Reuters data <UAH=>.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Mohammed Abbas) [20050601 091443 GMT]   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                 LATEST    PREV   LOCAL    LOCAL  \par                                 CLOSE  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                        PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par                                                 IN 2005\par  EUROPE/MIDDLE EAST/AFRICA\par  Czech Rep.   <EURCZK=>   30.37     30.35   -0.07    +0.12\par  Poland       <EURPLN=>    4.14      4.17   +0.55    -1.51\par  Hungary      <EURHUF=>  253.99    254.35   +0.14    -3.49\par  Slovakia     <EURSKK=>   39.15     39.11   -0.09    -1.11\par  Romania      <ROL=>     29,462    29,402   -0.20    -1.58\par  Ukraine      <UAH=>       5.07      5.08   +0.20    +4.78\par  South Africa <ZAR=>       6.81      6.79   -0.37   -17.21\par  Kenya        <KES=>      76.90     76.95   +0.07    +2.08\par  Israel       <ILS=>       4.41      4.41   -0.03    -2.12\par  \par  LATAM\par  Mexico       <MXN=>      10.89     10.88   -0.07    +2.23\par  Brazil       <BRL=>       2.39      2.41   +0.87   +11.19\par  Venezuela    <VEB=>    2144.60   2144.60   +0.00   -10.70\par  Chile        <CLP=>     583.00    583.00   +0.00    -4.72\par  \par  ASIA\par  Singapore    <SGD=>       1.67      1.67   -0.26    -2.26\par  Hong Kong    <HKD=>       7.78      7.78   -0.04    -0.13\par  Indonesia    <IDR=>      9,530     9,508   -0.23    -2.73\par  China        <CNY=>       8.28      8.28   +0.01    +0.00\par  Thailand     <THB=>      40.70     40.62   -0.20    -4.55\par  Philippines  <PHP=>      54.56     54.40   -0.29    +2.73\par  Taiwan       <TWD=>      31.34     31.34   +0.00    +1.28\par  South K