\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Ukraine's reformers spurn market in debt move  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Olena Horodetska and Andrew Hurst  \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2005\par 19:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KIEV/BELGRADE, May 23 (Reuters) - Ukraine acted on Monday to stave off a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis by banning foreigners from buying short-term treasuries, making it harder for speculators to bet on gains by the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The surprise decision followed a test of strength between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which allowed a sharp one-off gain in the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April but wants to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the market, which is betting on appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Capital has been sucked into Ukraine following last year's "orange revolution", which swept President Viktor Yushchenko to power promising to set the ex-Soviet state of nearly 50 million on a pro-western course.  \par \par \uc2 But, rather than embracing market reforms, Premier Yulia Tymoshenko's government has repeatedly resorted to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to steady \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fast-growing but vulnerable economy.  \par \par \uc2 She has already had a bruising run-in with Russian oil firms over fuel shortages, and the government remains at loggerheads over how to deal with dodgy privatisations dating back to former President Leonid Kuchma's time in office.  \par \par \uc2 Yushchenko has promised to publish a final list this week of 29 companies whose privatisations will be subjected to review, but analysts say infighting is eroding the reformers' political capital ahead of parliamentary elections due in early 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "Maybe we were too optimistic," commented Jed Sunden, publisher of the Korrespondent and Kiev Post weeklies. "Sadly, the Yushchenko administration's impulses so far have too often been consistent with Ukraine's socialist past."  \par \par \uc2 KEEP OUT  \par \par \uc2 Non-residents hold $1 billion in domestic government debt -- or half the outstanding amount -- creating upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  which persisted even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spike by 3 percent on April 20.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fought to defend a new target rate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> of 5.05 to the dollar with dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a near-daily basis, stoking inflation pressures.  \par \par \uc2 "A decision has been taken to set a limit -- a 365-day limit -- on non-residents' purchases. Non-residents do not have the right to invest in papers with 365-day or shorter maturities," said Halyna Pakhachuk, head of Finance Ministry debt department.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the decision appeared poorly coordinated, smacked of short-termism and failed to address the fundamental undervaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it was pegged for five years to a weakening dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "They are simply trying to limit inflows of hot money," said Tomasz Balamut at WestLB in Warsaw.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters see the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciating to 4.85 to the dollar by the end of this year.  \par \par \uc2 COORDINATION PROBLEM  \par \par \uc2 More serious is a rift between Yushchenko and the feisty Tymoshenko on a strategy for straightening out past privatisations and embarking on a fresh round of sell-offs.  \par \par \uc2 Yushchenko, who subordinates virtually every decision to his long-term objective of moving Ukraine out of Russia's shadow and closer to Europe, has promised a fast review of past sales made in questionable circumstances.  \par \par \uc2 He has promised this week to publish the hit-list of 29 companies whose sales will be reviewed -- but Tymoshenko has yet to even acknowledge its existence.  \par \par \uc2 "It is necessary to resolve (the privatisation issue) as soon as possible to declare an end to it and put businessmen's hearts at rest," Yushchenko said in a statement on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's business tycoons have nothing to fear if they pay fair value for assets sold to them on the cheap under the government of former President Leonid Kuchma, Deputy Prime Minister Olen Rybachuk said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are going to deal with them (business tycoons) very gently," Rybachuk said at the annual meeting of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD) in Belgrade.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-UKRAINE (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050523e15n001po\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
