\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's c.bank buys dollars at 5.01 for 6th day  \b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2005\par 13:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, May 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, buying dollars at 5.01 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar for the sixth day running as foreign demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained strong, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 On Apr. 20 the bank stunned the market fixing the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> at 5.05, up from 5.19 previously. The bank, which rigidly controls the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  ever since to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The market is awash with dollars. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no other choice at the moment but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said dollar supply to the market was driven by foreigners and Ukrainians willing to take out hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans in anticipation of further \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2 Western analysts and the International Monetary Fund see the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as undervalued. Some analysts predict it could rise to about 4.6-4.7 to the dollar by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 However, Ukrainian officials said \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  level at less than five \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar could hurt exporters, which account for about two-thirds of the economy and have driven the country's economic recovery in the last four years.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought about $4 billion on the interbank market trying to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise so far this year as exports of steel and chemicals boomed and foreign investors flocked in Ukraine's debt market.  \par \par \uc2 The bank bought about $2.6 billion over the entire 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "The only way out for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to open up the market, for everyone. Then we will probably see a slight outflow of capital and it could change the trend," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|MD|BNX|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050505e155000rd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
