\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rand dips, eyeing Barclays, C.Europe soft.  \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2005\par 12:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 26 (Reuters) - The South African rand slipped on Tuesday on investor concerns a big bank takeover might collapse, while the Polish zloty and Hungarian forint remained soft on global risk aversion and domestic political worries.  \par \par \uc2 The rand was pegged back versus the dollar on mounting concerns Britain's Barclays bank (BARC.L) might abandon its bid for a majority stake in South Africa's largest retail lender Absa (ASAJ.J) if minority shareholders demand a higher price.  \par \par \uc2 The rand had drawn strength from expectations of significant inflows into the country on the back of the deal, though analysts were optimistic the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not nose-dive if it did fall through.  \par \par \uc2 "The rand is going to stay in a 5.80-6.20 range against the dollar. I don't see much driving it out of that, even if the Barclays deal is off the agenda, it may go back to 6.20 but not much further," said Debbie Orgill, emerging markets analyst at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 By 0900 GMT, the rand traded slightly lower on the session at 6.07 per dollar .  \par \par \uc2 South African March inflation data failed to lift the mood, showing an upturn in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -targeted CPIX indicator to an annual rate of 3.6 percent in March, compared with February's record low 3.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "It does signal a turn in the inflation cycle. The last few months have been below expectations, but now we're likely to see it heading up to 4 percent," said Orgill.  \par \par \uc2 Orgill added that an interest rate cut at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next policy meeting in June looked unlikely, but said there could still be scope for easing later in the year, depending on oil prices and wage developments.  \par \par \uc2 The bank rocked the market with a surprise 50 basis point cut earlier in April, taking key rates to 7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 FORINT, ZLOTY SOFT  \par \par \uc2 An uncertain global environment, coupled with ongoing domestic political jitters in Poland, continued to lend a soft tone to the Hungarian forint and Polish zloty.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts also see a dovish interest rate bias across central Europe as potentially negative at a time when the Federal Reserve is mulling a faster pace of U.S. rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut key rates by a modest 25 basis points on Monday, taking them to 7.5 percent, but did urge caution in future due to global investor risk aversion and the risks to the forint from the country's economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "The decision did not have an impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the gradual erosion of the carry makes the forint more vulnerable to changes in sentiment," said Goldman Sachs analyst Istvan Zsoldos, in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The forint traded was marginally down at 249.15 to the euro .  \par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to follow with a 25 bp cut of its own at two-day monthly policy meeting, which started on Tuesday, though seven out of 20 economists polled by Reuters said the bank may opt for a bigger 50 bp cut.  \par \par \uc2 Of greater concern for investors is the political atmosphere ahead of elections, which must be held by October. A poll at the weekend showing the euro sceptic Law and Justice party taking the lead from the centre-right Civic Platform.  \par \par \uc2 Another potential negative for the zloty would be a 'no' vote from the French in a referendum on the EU constitution next month - a result which could cause a substantial delay in Poland's timetable for joining the euro. The zloty was off 0.3 percent at 4.20 per euro .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Tuesday, buying dollars at 5.01 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to stabilise the market, since it allowed a 3 percent appreciation in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's President Viktor Yuschenko said he was unhappy about last week's shock move, saying it undermined stability, but still forecast further gains thanks to a steady inflow of foreign investment.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050426e14q007sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
