\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to stem  \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia \highlight\uc2  surge.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Olena Horodetska  \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2005\par 18:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, April 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to rein in the country's soaraway \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  only a day after unleashing it in a move that shocked markets and sparked anger and confusion among politicians.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's 3 percent jump caught Ukraine's new reformist government cold while parliamentarians summoned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head and finance minister to explain the abandonment of a longstanding de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the National Bank of Ukraine bought dollars at a rate of 5.02 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a new five-year high - after stunning the market on Wednesday by letting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise strongly.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts saw more gains in store for the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they consider deeply undervalued, delivering a dose of anti-inflationary medicine which might have potentially harmful side-effects for Ukraine's export-led growth.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was a tool aimed at easing inflation pressure.  \par \par \uc2 "In its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes into consideration interests of all economic entities, in particular Ukraine's population with an aim to boost its consumer power," said the bank's First Deputy Chairman Anatoly Shapovalov.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko wants a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help bring down inflation, but her government sent mixed messages to a nation of 50 million wholly unprepared for the sudden move.  \par \par \uc2 "It is absolutely illogical to maintain an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate artificially because its price should be formed by the market," Interfax news agency quoted Tymoshenko as saying.  \par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Serhiy Teryokhin warned the leap could sow panic among Ukrainians, who like many people in the former Soviet Union hold much of their savings in U.S. dollars and may rush to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Parliament voted to summon \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Volodymyr Stelmakh and Finance Minister Viktor Pynzenyk on Friday to explain what was going on. Both have been out of sight so far.  \par \par \uc2 "We had a storm warning today in Kiev," Mykola Noshchenko, one deputy, told parliament. "It snowed in some regions - but Ukrainian families only started worrying when they got the storm warning from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What will happen tomorrow?"  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  move, to 5.05 to the dollar from 5.19 previously, came out of the blue.  \par \par \uc2 They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strict control of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade - it acts as a market clearing house and players are not allowed to sell and buy in a single session - meant the jump had shaken market confidence.  \par \par \uc2 "Their reasons are unclear. It is impossible to make any forecasts," one dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 Teryokhin said he had asked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Stelmakh about the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise but received no clear answer. "The economy ministry and the finance ministry do not understand what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing," he said.  \par \par \uc2 STILL UNDERVALUED  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained undervalued, in view of the weak dollar and bumper export earnings being raked in by key industries like steel which have widened the current account surplus to 11 percent of gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 "At the moment, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is a one-way bet given the sheer magnitude of the external surpluses," said Sergei Voloboev, emerging markets economist at CSFB in London.  \par \par \uc2 Lavish spending by the government ousted in last year's "Orange Revolution" has pushed inflation up to 13.5 percent - providing another reason for the market to bet on appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "Appreciation of 3 percent will have a very limited impact on inflation," said Lars Christensen, senior analyst at Danske Bank. "Appreciation of at least 15 percent is needed to bring inflation comfortably below 10 percent this year."  \par \par \uc2 Christensen noted that Russia and Kazakhstan - which like Ukraine have resource-based economies - had allowed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate by around 15 percent against the dollar since 2002. Ukraine had held the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  flat over that period.  \par \par \uc2 Hot portfolio inflows into Ukraine's domestic debt market may have provided the final trigger. A record $425 million in treasuries were snapped up at this week's auction - with up to 60 percent going to non-residents.  \par \par \uc2 Economic mismanagement could prove to be the undoing of Tymoshenko's three-month-old government, which faces a general election in early 2006, said Konrad Reuss, sovereign analyst at Standard & Poor's in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The way the Orange Revolution brought them in, the parliamentary election could sweep them out again," Reuss, just back from a visit to Ukraine, told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "Clearly there is a reform-minded government, but I am not really sure the government knows what it wants to do." (With reporting by Douglas Busvine in Moscow and Daniel Bases in Paris).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050421e14l00ba5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
