\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Ukraine budget gap soared $1 bln in run-up to vote.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Maria Golovnina  \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2004\par 19:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Ukraine's budget deficit jumped by $1 billion from almost nothing in the two months leading up to the political crisis triggered by November's disputed presidential election, official data showed on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Although the data did not show how the money was spent, analysts said the deficit probably pointed to a spending spree by outgoing President Leonid Kuchma and Prime Minister Viktor Yanukovich to woo voters in the Nov. 21 presidential run-off.  \par \par \uc2 When Yanukovich was declared winner of the election, hundreds of thousands of protesters poured into the streets in support of opposition leader Viktor Yushchenko, bringing much of Kiev to a halt. The Supreme Court has declared the poll invalid and ordered a re-run for Dec. 26.  \par \par \uc2 Kuchma has repeatedly warned Ukrainians that the economy was on the brink of collapse, saying the protests were to blame. His remarks provoked a run on banks, drove up consumer prices and fuelled demand for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 But Tuesday's data released by the State Treasury Department showed the state budget started to suffer long before the political crisis erupted.  \par \par \uc2 The January-October deficit widened to 5.608 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  ($1.06 billion) from 59.9 million \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first eight months of the year, the statement said without elaboration.  \par \par \uc2 "Budget data for October shows massive pork-barrelling in the run-up to the presidential election by the Kuchma/Yanukovich administration," said Timothy Ash, emerging Europe analyst at Bear Stearns.  \par \par \uc2 "Whoever takes power after this debacle will need to rein in the budget, as a major priority," Ash said.  \par \par \uc2 By law, the 2004 deficit must not exceed 6.975 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We don't know what they spent it on right now. but yes, clearly the government was probably using the budget to gain favours here and there in the run-up to the election," Zsolt Pap, emerging market analyst at ABN AMRO in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The first round of the presidential election was at the end of October and we now have figures for the first 10 months. The deterioration (in the budget) happened in September and October, so it is reasonable to assume some of the spending is election related."  \par \par \uc2 Separate figures from Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday showed the country's gross hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $9.9 billion on Dec. 3 from $10.3 billion a week earlier, mainly due to higher market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan on Tuesday recommended investors cut exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign debt to "marketweight" from "overweight" amid concerns that political instability would linger after the country's electoral crisis is resolved.  \par \par \uc2 "In our view, the main risks are that political uncertainty will continue even if Yushchenko wins the new elections," JPMorgan said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 The protesters have vowed to maintain a blockade of government buildings until they see Yushchenko inaugurated as president.  \par \par \uc2 TROUBLE TO COME  \par \par \uc2 Although official macroeconomic data has yet to show any major fallout from the crisis, economists warned the trouble could lie ahead.  \par \par \uc2 Kuchma's remark late last month that Ukraine's financial system could collapse "like a house of cards" may have triggered a fresh surge in consumer prices after inflation hit a record 2.2 percent in October.  \par \par \uc2 The comment came too late to affect November data, released on Monday, but could push December inflation above October's record.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts believe Ukraine, where the average monthly wage is the equivalent of about $100, can withstand the crisis until the end of December but warned investors that further turmoil could jolt the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly called on Ukrainians to remain calm and urged the government to take action to contain price rises. Analysts, however, say 2004 inflation could be far above the 12 percent forecast by the bank. (Additional reporting by Natalia Zinets, Greg Brosnan in New York and Sabyasachi Mitra and Daniel Bases in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041207e0c700and\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
