\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-IMF encouraged by Ukraine growth. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mark Egan \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2001\par 16:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Jan 19 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund on Friday said it was encouraged by economic progress in Ukraine and urged the former Soviet state to control its money supply to lower its inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  In its annual assessment of the Ukraine economy, the Washington-based lender forecast that inflation hit about 27.1 percent last year. On a more positive note, economic growth reached 4.2 percent in 2000, marking the first year of positive growth in almost a decade of independence. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's verdict on Ukraine was fairly upbeat, saying it, "welcomed the recent improvements in economic policy implementation and endorsed Ukraine's overall strategy based on a sound budget, tight monetary policy and supportive structural reforms." \par \par \uc2  The fund told Ukraine, which gained independence in August 1991, to stay the course of its program for economic stability and structural reforms, saying it was vital to help the former Soviet state make a successful transition to a market economy. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said the nominal exchange rate remained stable in 2000 but that inflation was higher than expected, eroding improvements in competitiveness achieved in 1999. With that in mind, the IMF urged the Bank of Ukraine, "Not to target any particular level of the exchange rate and to be prepared to accept greater exchange-rate flexibility in both directions." \par \par \uc2  Keeping monetary aggregates, or total money supply, under control would help lower inflation, the fund noted. \par \par \uc2  Some IMF directors were critical of the pace of privatization in Ukraine, noting that no large enterprise had yet been sold in an open and transparent bidding procedure. The IMF said it was essential Ukraine press ahead more rapidly with structural reforms to reduce the role of the state in the economy and bolster private sector confidence and investment. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said any privatization receipts above expected amounts should be used for debt reduction. It also said that efforts should be made to broaden the tax base before any tax cuts to make the overall effect on revenues neutral. \par \par \uc2  The lender said Ukraine's current account surplus will likely narrow in 2001, reflecting more moderate export growth and the full-year impact of higher energy imports. \par \par \uc2  The improved growth performance in 2000 stemmed largely from the real effective exchange-rate depreciation of the the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in 1999, better-than-expected growth in Russia and recovery in other export markets particularly in metals. \par \par \uc2  Ukraine's exchange rate was broadly stable in 2000 as it depreciated by 34 percent against the U.S. dollar. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which bought large amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2000, helped push inflation up to 27.1 percent from 19 percent in the previous year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx1j00ia0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
